  "body": "A failed rollout of the new Google subject-binding handler could restore the prior binary or leave untouched replicas authorizing by email. The release runner now requires a compatible prior image or an explicit verified `--recovery-sha` before production mutation, and recovers every unsafe replica once any deployment has been attempted.\n\nThe fallback must be a distinct reviewed ancestor containing the authentication safety commit, with identical migration manifests and expanded SQL checksums. Preflight verifies immutable target and recovery images. Recovery uses the guarded deploy/smoke path, preserves operational flags and provider bindings, retains the original canary error, continues after individual recovery failures and records them. A failure before any deploy attempt causes no machine recovery.\n\nValidation: 74 release tests passed, including the actual fleet recovery loop for both canary choices/all prior combinations and injected recovery failure. The full pinned TLA+/Alloy runner passed. Four recovery configurations pass (38/31/35/38 distinct states); two negative controls reproduce unsafe restoration and partial-fleet recovery. The model assumes successful recovery commands and does not certify temporary mixed-fleet intervals or cloud availability. Local Git/manifest plans accept5c115 withf1ff recovery and reject2f01 migration mismatch. No production failure or rollback was induced.\n\nResolves UX-260917-036. Merge requires exact-head approval and all applicable checks. Production rollout additionally requires the reviewed runner, immutable compatible recovery artifact, fresh snapshot, release lease, canary/fleet verification and observation. Historical migrations and authentication handlers are unchanged.\n",
